// `rt`: the runtime compiled programs link, in Grasm (docs/self-hosting.md
// §6.7, §6.12, stage S5), against today's ABI -- the C entry points of
// `libgrasm_rt_abi.a` and the memory layouts its values have. It defines all
// of them, and a program is linked with it in the archive's place:
//
//     grasm tir compiler/rt/src/main.gr > rt.tir
//     s3 rt.tir -o rt.o --runtime
//     grasm-link <program objects> -interpose rt.o ...
//
// Each `export fn grt_<name>` here is the program's `grasm_<name>`: the
// linker binds the program's references to it (`-interpose`), and the
// backend names the runtime's own calls of one -- `/` and `%` checking their
// divisor -- by its `grt_` name (`compiler/mir`). It calls nothing of the
// Rust runtime's: `scripts/rtswap.sh` holds it to `families.txt`, and to
// that. Its poisoned build, which keeps every freed box out of circulation,
// is this package with `build.gr` saying so.
//
// The rules the code keeps:
//
//   * no Grasm heap value -- no `str`, list, map, struct or closure: only
//     the layout types and raw memory, so nothing here calls the runtime
//     it is part of to make one;
//   * state only through the runtime dialect, which this package alone may
//     use (`runtime = true`, [T-RUNTIME], docs/self-hosting.md §5.6): what is
//     one per process in `process_local` cells, every access to one atomic
//     or under a lock built from atomics, as `scripts/rtlint.py` requires;
//     what is one per thread in its own `thread_local` cells, which the Grasm
//     backend makes Mach-O thread-local variables, never the program's block;
//   * fixed text is static bytes (`mem_static`): a message is never a value
//     the runtime would have to make to say it;
//   * every layout it reads or writes is in `layout.gr` and checked against
//     the Rust runtime's by `scripts/rtlayout.sh`.
//
// The families, each in its own module:
//
//     math     integer power, `%` on floats, abs/min/max, rounding, libm
//     panic    the panics' messages
//     io       the output buffers, and the ways out of a program
//     way      a panic with the program's text; the error `main` returns
//     fmt      integers and floats as text, bit-identical to Rust's `{}`
//     out      `print`'s pieces
//     numstr   numbers as string values and as interpolated pieces
//     strread  strings read: length in bytes, emptiness, equality, order
//     val      reference counting
//     release  the release walk, for every payload
//     alloc    the box allocator: pools, live counts, interned boxes
//     boxes    scalars boxed and unboxed, and the layout check
//     region   regions opened and swept
//     leak     how many boxes are live, and the leak check
//     vals     value structs released and copied by their descriptor
//     keys, maps, equal, display, structs, refs, funcs, bytes, lists,
//     strings, case, numparse, fiber, tasks    the values' families
//     journal  record and replay
//     files, clock, envs, cmem, net, proc      the system families
//     par      the parallel workers
//
// and those that serve them: `layout`, `sys` (the C library's memory),
// `fsys` (files at the system calls), `sysv` (what the system families
// answer with) and `build` (which build this is).
import math
import panic
import layout
import fmt
import out
import numstr
import strread
import val
import release
import alloc
import boxes
import region
import leak
import vals
import keys
import maps
import equal
import display
import structs
import refs
import funcs
import bytes
import lists
import strings
import case
import numparse
import fiber
import tasks
import io
import way
import sysv
import fsys
import journal
import files
import clock
import envs
import cmem
import net
import proc
import par
import build
import sys
